      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cleburne County was named for Confederate general Patrick Cleburne of Arkansas, who fell at the Battle of Franklin in 1864.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/cleburne-county/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The county was created on December 6 1866 from portions of Randolph, Talladega, and Calhoun counties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/cleburne-county/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1882 the Georgia‑Pacific Railroad extended to Heflin, spurring local commerce and growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/cleburne-county/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After a 1905 election, Heflin became the county seat, a decision upheld by the Supreme Court in 1906.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleburne_County%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cleburne’s dense forests made it an early hub for timber and pulpwood industries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/cleburne-county/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
